\section{Common mistakes}{

\strong{Cells read \verb{98 ||| 46 (32, 63)}.} You are on a version before the
split; upgrade. \verb{|||} is the internal separator \code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}}
writes between the count and the statistic, never intended to reach a
rendered table.

\strong{"\code{tbl} was not built with the \verb{\{N_nonmiss\} ||| \{stat\}} convention."}
Only some cells carry the separator, which means \code{statistic} was set
for part of the table. Splitting would leave the rest blank, so it is
rejected. Either apply the convention to every variable or to none;
\code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}} does the former for you.

\strong{"\code{font_size} must be 11 or 12."} House rule 5. Use \code{11} only for
wide tables.

\strong{"\code{font} and \code{font_size} set the report style."} You passed one with
\code{style = "manuscript"}. That style sets no font, so the table takes the
font of the document it is saved into. Drop the argument, or use
\code{style = "report"}.

\strong{The compare layout refuses a \code{continuous_stat = "both"} table.}
That table has a column for the median and another for the mean and
SD, which the compare layout's one statistic column per group cannot
lay out. Build the table with \code{"median"} or \code{"mean"}, or draw it with
\code{layout = "describe"} or the report style, which show both.

\strong{The manuscript style refuses a table not built by
\code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}}.} The layout reads the overall count and the
grouping's name that function records. Build the table with it.

\strong{"\code{layout} chooses between the manuscript style's layouts."} You
named a layout with the report style, which has one. Drop \code{layout}, or
add \code{style = "manuscript"}.
}

